articles.content_en:
Where a resolution of the shareholders' meeting, general meeting of shareholders, or board of directors falls under any of the following circumstances, and a party asserts that the resolution has not been established, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) The company did not convene a meeting, except where, pursuant to Article 37(2) of the Company Law or the provisions of the company's articles of association, a decision may be made directly without convening a shareholders' meeting or general meeting of shareholders, and the decision document has been signed and sealed by all shareholders;
(2) The meeting did not vote on the matters of the resolution;
(3) The number of persons attending the meeting or the voting rights held by shareholders do not conform to the provisions of the Company Law or the company's articles of association;
(4) The voting result of the meeting did not reach the adoption ratio required by the Company Law or the company's articles of association;
(5) Other circumstances causing the resolution not to be established.

Where a limited liability company has evidence proving that a shareholder falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall find that the shareholder has an "improper purpose" as prescribed in Article 33(2) of the Company Law:
(1) The shareholder operates, for his or her own account or for another person, a business that has a substantive competitive relationship with the company's principal business, except where the company's articles of association provide otherwise or all shareholders agree otherwise;
(2) The shareholder inspects the company's accounting books for the purpose of disclosing relevant information to others, which may damage the company's lawful interests;
(3) Within three years before the date of the shareholder's request to the company for inspection, the shareholder has, through inspecting the company's accounting books, disclosed relevant information to others and damaged the company's lawful interests;
(4) Other circumstances in which the shareholder has an improper purpose.
